  "worked_solution": "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int -\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(4)=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{4}{(4)}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =1+C \\\\ C & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{4}{t}-\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{t}-\\frac{2}{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{t} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ t & =\\frac{12}{2} \\\\ t & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary when $t=6\\,\\text{s}.$",
